The closest prior synthesis to the present manuscript is the review of Yang and Lee [ 1 ], which organises the field primarily through hardening phenomena and modelling concepts. The present article deliberately shifts the organising axis. Rather than attempting a broader census of model variants, it asks four engineering questions: which experimentally observed path-change effects need to be captured, which constitutive ingredients are minimally required, what test information is needed to identify those ingredients, and what implementation consequences follow for FE robustness. Table  1  makes this difference explicit by positioning the present review against selected prior syntheses and reference texts. The point is not that earlier contributions neglected engineering concerns entirely, but that calibration burden, identifiability, model-selection logic and FE deployment were seldom the primary organising principles of the review itself. Table 1 Positioning of the present review relative to selected prior syntheses and reference texts. Symbols denote central coverage ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
