Reverse loading and cyclic tension–compression tests are indispensable for quantifying kinematic hardening, Bauschinger effects and cyclic stabilisation, and for discriminating between different families of combined hardening models [ 10 ,  32 ,  38 ,  48 ,  49 ]. Non-proportional and multi-step loading paths, implemented either in cruciform specimens or in thin-walled tubes under combined internal pressure, axial load and torsion, expose cross-hardening and distortional hardening effects that are largely invisible in proportional tests [ 1 ,  32 ]. Through-thickness or out-of-plane tests (when available) finally address three-dimensional aspects of anisotropy and are relevant for processes involving bending and stretching, as well as for calibrating fully three-dimensional anisotropic yield criteria [ 4 ,  24 ]. Taken together, these tests form a toolbox from which tailored programmes can be designed to identify and validate the constitutive models discussed in the following sections. Table 2 Summary of experimental tests for characterising deformation-induced anisotropic hardening in sheet and tubular metallic materials. The table lists representative test types, their main observables and the aspects of anisotropy and hardening that they constrain [ 4 ,  7 ,  25 ] Test type Main observables Aspects constrained Uniaxial tension (RD, TD, 45 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
