ng under cyclic loading Multiple backstress components with dynamic recovery or recall terms; mixed isotropic– kinematic evolution tailored to cyclic path memory Advanced kinematic hardening models with multiple backstress components and recall terms (multi-component Chaboche, AbdelKarim–Ohno-type); combined isotropic–kinematic models tuned for ratchetting [ 10 ,  59 ,  84 ] Symmetric and asymmetric strain-controlled cyclic tests at different mean stress levels; multi-level cyclic tests to capture transient behaviour and ratchetting under non-zero mean stress Yield-locus distortion under strain-path changes Distortional or directional shape evolution of the locus, often superposed with isotropic expansion and kinematic translation Distortional hardening models (François-type yield-surface distortion, Feigenbaum–Dafalias directional distortion, e-HAH and related high-anisotropic hardening models); often combined with isotropic and kinematic components Multi-axial yield-locus measurements after different pre-strain levels (uniaxial, plane strain, equi-biaxial); tension–compression and two-step non-proportional loading paths to trigger distortion and validate the evolution of shape in the deviatoric plane [ 11 ,  12 ,  51 ,  55 ,  56 ] Cross-hardening and non-proportional hardening under orthogonal or multi-step loading Path-dependent directional hardening, structure-tensor effects and sometimes non-coaxial or higher-order internal variables Models with direction-dependent or path-dependent hardening internal variables (e.g. Teodosiu–Hu class, HAH-type models with non-proportional loading terms, enhanced kinematic/distortional formulations with additional structure tensors) [ 1 ,  51 ,  60 ,  97 ,  98 ] Two- and three-step loading paths with cross-loading or orthogonal reloading (e.g. tension in RD followed by tension or shear in TD); combined with yield-locus measurements to expose cross-hardening and non-proportional hardening effects [ 50 ,  60 ] Path-dependent evolution of  r -values and planar anisotropy Explicit evolution of anisotropy coefficients or interpolated yield surfaces; mixed hardening linked to texture and loading-path history Mixed kinematic/distortional hardening within anisotropic yield functions, sometimes augmented by interpolation schemes between yield functions at different strain levels (e.g. Yoshida-type interpolation, path-dependent anisotropic hardening); CP-informed phenomenological models with evolving anisotropy parameters [ 20 ,  54 ,  57 ,  58 ,  61 ] Uniaxial tests in multiple in-plane directions at several strain levels (including after strain-path changes); complementary CP or CPFE simulations used as a virtual laboratory to generate synthetic  r -value data for calibration when experiments are limited

