The most frequent implementation issues, their typical triggers, and practical remedies are summarised in Table  7 . Table 7 Frequent numerical issues in the FE implementation of anisotropic hardening models and practical remedies. The table is intentionally diagnostic rather than exhaustive Issue Typical trigger Observable symptom Practical remedy Elastic–plastic switching chatter Very small increments, tight tolerances, non-smooth yield loci Alternation between elastic and plastic branches; noisy incremental response Use a robust yield tolerance, active-set logic and adaptive substepping near first yield. Confirm that the oscillation is not created by an oversized numerical viscosity. Local Newton stagnation Severe path change, poorly scaled residuals, strongly coupled backstress/distortional variables Residual norm stalls or oscillates; excessive iteration count Scale residual blocks, use a line search or damping strategy, and reparameterise internal variables if magnitudes differ by orders of magnitude. Ill-conditioned local Jacobian or tangent Reduced yield-surface curvature, repeated eigenvalues, incomplete linearisation Loss of quadratic convergence; abrupt cutback requests from the global solver Check derivative consistency, regularise near singular curvature states, and compare the hand-coded tangent against finite-difference or automatic-differentiation benchmarks at the material-point level. Inadmissible parameter set Unconstrained optimisation, over-flexible model family, poor initial guess Non-convex yield locus, negative dissipation, algorithm breakdown Impose admissibility constraints during optimisation, restrict the parameter space to structurally justified ranges, and reject parameter sets that fail convexity or dissipation checks before FE deployment. Incomplete non-associated implementation Yield function and plastic potential differentiated inconsistently Correct stress levels but wrong plastic-flow direction or  r -value evolution; poor reverse-loading convergence Linearise yield and plastic-potential derivatives separately and verify proportional, reverse, and orthogonal loading paths against a reference material-point implementation. Plane-stress inconsistency Shell formulation solved by ex-post condensation of  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
